.TH SPLIT 1
.SH NAME
split \- split a file into pieces, in Limbo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B split
[
.BI -n\  num
]
[
.BI -e\  exp
]
[
.BI -f\  stem
]
[
.BI -s\  suff
]
[
.B -x
]
[
.B -i
]
[
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Split
is a structural Limbo translation of Plan 9's
.IR split (1),
not a behavioral workalike \(em see
.B doc/plan9-port.md
in the source tree for the distinction, and for a real, confirmed
divergence between two Plan 9 lineages on this specific file that this
translation had to resolve (see BUGS). It lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in.
.PP
With no
.I file
argument, standard input is split; otherwise
.I file
is.
.PP
In its default mode,
.I split
writes every
.I num
lines (default 1000, set by
.B -n
or its historic synonym
.BR -l )
of input to a new file, named by
.I stem
(default
.BR x )
followed by a two-letter suffix starting at
.BR aa ,
.BR ab ,
.BR ac ,
\&... up to
.BR zz ;
running out of suffixes stops splitting (reported once to standard
error) rather than erroring.
.PP
.BI -e\  exp
switches to pattern mode instead: each input line is checked against
the regular expression
.IR exp .
If
.I exp
has a parenthesized group, a match starts a new file named by the
matched text of that group (plus
.BR -s 's
suffix, if given) \(em
.I stem
is not used at all in this case. Without a group, a match instead
starts a new
.I stem
-named file the same way the default mode does (so
.B -s
has no effect there either \(em it only ever applies to a group-derived
name). Either way the matching line itself is included in the new
file, unless
.B -x
is given, which drops it from both the old and new file's output.
.B -i
makes the match (and, when a group is used, the resulting filename)
case-insensitive by folding both the pattern and each line to lower
case before comparing.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/split.b
(translated from
.BR sys/src/cmd/split.c )
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR splittest (1)
.SH BUGS
This translation is of
.BR plan9port 's
copy of
.BR split.c ,
not 9front's - the two have genuinely diverged on this file (checked by
diffing them, not assumed). 9front's copy opens a real default output
file before reading any input in pattern mode; plan9port's does not, so
in plan9port (and this translation) any input line
.I before
the first pattern match is silently dropped \(em no output file is open
yet to receive it, and nothing reports this. Confirmed directly against
the real plan9port binary. A live 9front binary to confirm its own
(probably different, non-dropping) behaviour directly was not available
on this machine (its plan9port libraries are x86_64-only, an
environment limit); the choice to translate plan9port's copy here
follows explicit direction to do so when a 9front/plan9port divergence
cannot be independently verified.
.PP
.BR openf ()'s
own error message on failing to create an output file says
.RB \(lq grep \(rq,
not
.RB \(lq split \(rq
\(em a real copy-paste typo in the original, preserved rather than
corrected.
.PP
Regular-expression matching and submatch extraction use this tree's own
.IR regex (2)
module, an existing Inferno regex engine, not Plan 9's
.IR regexp (2)
reimplemented \(em the two are different implementations of a similar
grammar, not guaranteed to agree on every edge case, though ordinary
patterns (anchors, groups, character classes) behave the same.
